WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
In the last video, you saw what a single hidden layer neural network looks like.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.320
In this video, let's go through the details of exactly how this neural network computes

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:10.320
its outputs.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.760
What you see is that it's like logistic regression, but repeated a lot of times.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:16.760
Let's take a look.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.880
So this is what a two-layer neural network looks like.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:24.160
Let's go more deeply into exactly what this neural network computes.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:29.240
Now we've said before that logistic regression, the circle in logistic regression, really

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.000
represents two steps of computation.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.840
First, you compute Z as follows.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:37.200
And then second, you compute the activation as a sigmoid function of Z.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.920
So a neural network just does this a lot more times.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.600
Let's start by focusing on just one of the nodes in the hidden layer.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:46.480
Let's look at the first node in the hidden layer.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.200
So I've grayed out the other nodes for now.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:52.800
So similar to logistic regression on the left, this node in the hidden layer does

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.600
two steps of computation.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:01.200
The first step, and think of as the left half of this node, it computes Z equals W transpose

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:07.600
X plus B. And the notation we'll use is these are all quantities associated with the first

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:08.600
hidden layer.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:10.880
So that's why we have a bunch of square brackets there.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.760
And this is the first node in the hidden layer.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.840
So that's why we'll have a subscript 1 over there.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:18.080
So first it does that.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:24.240
And then the second step is it computes A11 equals sigmoid of Z11.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:31.440
So for both Z and A, the notation convention is that A, L, I, the L here in superscript

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.880
square brackets refers to the layer number, and the I subscript here refers to the nodes

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:37.880
in that layer.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:42.160
So the node we've been looking at is layer 1, that is the hidden layer node 1.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:46.080
So that's why the superscript and subscripts were both 1, 1.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:51.160
So that little circle, that first node in the neural network represents carrying out these

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:52.760
two steps of computation.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:56.160
Now let's look at the second node in the neural network, or the second node in the

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.960
hidden layer of the neural network.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.440
Similar to the logistic regression unit on the left, this little circle represents two

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:04.880
steps of computation.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:10.000
The first step is it computes Z. This is still layer 1, but now it's the second node

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.760
equals W transpose X plus B12.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.080
And then A12 equals sigmoid of Z12.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:23.560
Feel free to pause the video if you want, but you can double check that the superscript

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:28.560
and subscript notation is consistent with what we had written here above in purple.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.200
So we'll talk through the first two hidden units in the neural network.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.080
Hidden units 3 and 4 also represent similar computations.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:42.920
So now let me take this pair of equations and this pair of equations, and let's copy

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:44.240
them to the next slide.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:48.760
So here's our neural network, and here's the first, and here's the second equations.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:54.240
They were worked out previously for the first and the second hidden units.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.840
If you then go through and write out the corresponding equations for the third and

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.520
fourth hidden units, you get the following.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:03.960
And just make sure this notation is clear.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:06.720
This is the vector W11.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.160
This is a vector transpose times X.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:12.160
So that's what the superscript T there represents.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:13.560
This is a vector transpose.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.280
Now as you might have guessed, if you're actually implementing a neural network,

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.400
doing this with a for loop seems really inefficient.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.200
So what we're going to do is take these four equations and vectorize.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:29.160
So we're gonna start by showing how to compute Z as a vector.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:30.960
It turns out you could do it as follows.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.920
Let me take these W's and stack them into a matrix.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:39.480
Then you have W11 transpose, so that's a row vector.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.280
This is a column vector transpose gives you a row vector.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:49.000
Then W12 transpose, W13 transpose, W14 transpose.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:54.600
And so this, by stacking those four W vectors together, you end up with a matrix.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:59.040
So another way to think of this is that we have four logistic regression units there.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:03.960
And each of the logistic regression units has a corresponding parameter vector W.

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:06.160
And by stacking those four vectors together,

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:09.040
you end up with this four by three matrix.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:13.640
So if you then take this matrix and multiply it by your input features,

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:18.680
X1, X2, X3, you end up with, by how matrix multiplication works,

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:25.520
you end up with W11 transpose X, W12 transpose X, W3,

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.760
1 transpose X, W4, 1 transpose X.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:31.120
And then let's not forget the B's.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:35.720
So we now add to this a vector B1, 1, B12,

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:39.360
B13, B14.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.840
So that's basically this.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:46.280
Then this is B11, B12, B13, B14.

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:51.640
And so you see that each of the four rows of this outcome correspond exactly to each

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:55.920
of these four rows, each of these four quantities that we had above.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:01.280
So in other words, we've just shown that this thing is therefore equal to Z11,

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:05.560
Z12, Z13, Z14, as defined here.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:09.800
And maybe not surprisingly, we're going to call this whole thing the vector Z1,

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:15.160
which is taken by stacking up these individuals of Z's into a column vector.

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:20.240
When we're vectorizing, one of the rules of thumb that might help you navigate this

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:23.960
is that when we have different nodes in a layer, we'll stack them vertically.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:28.800
So that's why when you have Z11 through Z14, those corresponded to four different

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:30.560
nodes in the hidden layer.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:34.840
And so we stacked these four numbers vertically to form the vector Z1.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:39.280
And to use one more piece of notation, this 4 by 3 matrix here,

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:44.680
which we obtained by stacking the lowercase, you know, W11, W12, and so on,

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:48.040
we're going to call this matrix W1.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:52.600
And similarly, this vector we're going to call B superscript 1 square bracket.

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:54.600
And so this is a 4 by 1 vector.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:59.560
So now we've computed Z using this vector matrix notation.

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:03.160
The last thing we need to do is also compute these values of A.

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:08.520
And so probably won't surprise you to see that we're going to define A1 as just

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:13.080
stacking together those activation values, A11 through A14.

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:17.960
So just take these four values and stack them together in a vector called A1.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:23.200
And this is going to be sigmoid of Z1, where this now has been implementation

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:27.040
of the sigmoid function that takes in the four elements of Z and

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:30.640
applies the sigmoid function elements-wise to it.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:37.360
So just to recap, we've figured out that Z1 is equal to W1 times the vector X

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:41.960
plus the vector B1, and A1 is sigmoid times Z1.

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:44.040
Let's just copy this to the next slide.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:46.920
And what we see is that for the first layer of the neural network,

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:52.040
given an input X, we have that Z1 is equal to W1 times X plus B1,

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:55.560
and A1 is sigmoid of Z1.

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:59.040
And the dimensions of this are 4 by 1 equals,

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:05.240
this was a 4 by 3 matrix times a 3 by 1 vector plus a 4 by 1 vector B.

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:08.000
And this is 4 by 1, same dimension as Z.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.840
And remember that we said X is equal to A0, right?

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:16.560
Just like Y hat is also equal to A2.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:21.040
So if you want, you can actually take this X and replace it with A0.

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:25.440
Since A0 is, if you want, is an alias for the vector of input features X.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:27.240
Now, through a similar derivation,

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:31.360
you can figure out that the representation for the next layer

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:35.640
can also be written similarly, where what the output layer does

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:40.840
is it has associated with it, set the parameters W2 and B2.

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:44.560
So W2 in this case is going to be a 1 by 4 matrix,

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:46.960
and B2 is just a real number as 1 by 1.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:50.040
And so Z2 is going to be a real number,

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:52.120
which is right as a 1 by 1 matrix.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:56.720
It's going to be a 1 by 4 thing times A was 4 by 1 plus B2

00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:57.680
is 1 by 1.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:07:59.760
And so this gives you just a real number.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:02.120
And if you think of this loss output unit

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:04.880
as just being analogous to logistic regression, which

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:10.440
had parameters W and B, W really plays an analogous role

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:12.520
to W2 transpose.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:16.800
So W2 is really W transpose, and B is equal to B2.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:20.320
So if you were to cover up the left of this network

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:22.960
and ignore all that for now, then this is just,

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:26.280
this loss output unit is a lot like logistic regression,

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:29.480
except that instead of writing the parameters as W and B,

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:34.120
we're writing them as W2 and B2 with dimensions 1 by 4

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:35.880
and 1 by 1.

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:38.640
So just to recap, for logistic regression,

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:41.640
to implement the output or to implement prediction,

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:44.920
you compute Z equals W transpose X plus B,

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:51.240
and A, or Y hat, equals A equals sigmoid of Z.

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:54.280
When you have a neural network with one hidden layer,

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:56.760
what you need to implement to compute this output

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:59.040
is just these four equations.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:02.240
And you can think of this as a vectorized implementation

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:06.800
of computing the output of first these four logistic regression

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:08.000
units in the hidden layer.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:09.120
That's what this does.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:12.520
And then this logistic regression in the output layer,

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:13.880
which is what this does.

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:15.640
I hope this description made sense,

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:18.160
but the takeaway is to compute the output

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:19.920
of this neural network, all you need

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:21.880
is those four lines of code.

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:25.680
So now you've seen how, given a single input feature vector

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:27.680
X, you can, with four lines of code,

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:30.440
compute the output of this neural network.

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:32.520
Similar to what we did for logistic regression,

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:35.480
we'll also want to vectorize across multiple training

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:36.320
examples.

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:39.880
And we'll see that by stacking up training examples

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:41.640
in different columns in the matrix,

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:43.760
with just slight modification to this,

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:46.680
you also, similar to what you saw in logistic regression,

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:50.120
be able to compute the output of this neural network,

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:52.040
not just on one example at a time,

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:55.280
but on your, say, your entire training set at a time.

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:57.891
So let's see the details of that in the next video.
